e is that there is really a  genocide going on in in eastern ukraine  and russia has a responsibility to  protect its own people i think that's  the major point we'll have to see what  comes out of it in the next days  and as part of that the russian  parliament has called on president putin  to recognize the separatist regions of  donetsk and luhansk  as independent how would that change  things  well it's in putin's hand whether he is  to sign this into law and then if this  happens then of course russia considers  to have the not only moral but also  legal right to intervene in ukraine and  this might change things just a bit more  for the worse  if one of russia's goals has been to  frustrate ukraine's attempts to join  nato then permanently basing troops at  that border at its border with ukraine  might achieve that is that feasible in  the long term  well it depends russia surely could  deploy material to the border for  a very long time  i'm not sure whether it would be  possible to deploy troops  in this region for an indefinite time  they they have to reorganize and you  only can live in tents for so long even  if russian soldiers most probably are a  bit more robust than let's say western  european soldiers in that regard but i i  would think that at some point russia  has to take a decision whether this is a  really  big amassing of troops will stay  thank you for that that's very clear uh  defense and security journalist  thomas viggold  you're welcome  let's get more from dw's chief  international editor richard walker  welcome richard so some signs of some  movement but it sounds like it's too  early to breathe a sigh of relief  yeah i think that's right phil i mean  first of all of course it's going to  take a while to ascertain whether those  troops are really are moving what  numbers of troops are moving back where  are they moving back to are they moving  back to base or are they moving to other  forward positions from which that they  also  potentially pose a threat so there's  there are those questions still to be  answered but  still  many people have been clinging on to  that moment yesterday when this  announcement came out as is the first  sort of  de-escalatory turn  that this crisis has taken in many weeks  of ratcheting up of steady escalation  but we should stress that also yesterday  some other escalatory incidents did  happen we saw in the report there this  vote in the duma in the russian  parliament  to call on the kremlin to recognize  those rebel regions in eastern ukraine  that would be a hugely disruptive move  if the russians moved ahead with that  that would throw the whole crisis into a  new level of dangerousness  plus also there was a significant cyber  attack yesterday that took place against  various ukrainian institutions now it  always takes a while to attribute who  actually initiated a cyber attack but of  course there'll be very strong  suspicions that russia is involved given  the circumstances that we're in at the  moment plus you have a very large number  of troops certainly still in the region  around ukraine so all in all this is  still very volatile the situation  now the german chancellor olaf schultz  was in moscow yesterday following his  talks in kiev the day before did he  achieve anything  well i think he'll be reasonably  satisfied with how the trip went um i  think on two levels essentially partly  just the opportunity for him to get his  message across not just in public in the  way that we saw in the press conferences  in the report just there but also in  private several hours of getting his  message across to vladimir putin and  indicating that the western alliance is  united on this situation  and then otherwise simply the pledge  from the russian side came out to keep  talking some of the more positive noises  from vladimir putin on his interest in  talking coming out from the press  conference yesterday so i think all in  all uh schultz will be reasonably  content with how things went  uh and one of mr putin's biggest demands  is that uh nato halts its expansion  eastwards especially uh regarding  ukraine which of course nato has  rejected so how do we resolve that how  do they resolve that clash of positions  yeah well this is one of the naughtiest  questions and any talks going ahead are  really going to find difficulty  resolving this and schultz voicing some  frustration almost about the situation  sort of posing the question why are the  russians sort of instigating this crisis  over a question which is really nobody  believes that ukraine is is about to  join nato at any time soon  um and there have been some questions  about whether some sort of formula could  be found to reassure the russians that  the ukrainian membership of nato is not  imminent but one journalist  posed that question to vladimir putin  asked if he would accept something like  that and he he rejected that quite  angrily sort of saying uh when would it  then join or if it's not tomorrow when  might it be so rejecting this idea quite  quite strongly and the problem of all of  this is that vladimir putin essentially  has a strong incentive out of all of  that just to keep up this kind of level  of pressure on ukraine because if he  can't get a pledge from nato that it  won't expand  then this kind of disruption this  constant harassment of ukraine is a de  facto achievement of that goal stopping  it ever being in a position where it  could join nato all right thanks for  that richard richard walker is dw's  chief international editor
 

